### Identity Federation
- Uses a centralized trusted authenticator
- Authentication capabitlities are removed from individual apps, they rely on centralized authenticator for authentication
- A trusted authenticator or identity provider can span organizations
- It's nothing but options available in most of the modern day apps to sign in with google, microsoft, facebook accounts instead of creating a new sign up account

Identity and Resource providers:
- Identity provider (IDP), this is centralized authentication or directory service, it can be on-premises or cloud based. Cloud based is like using Amazon ID, google, facebook cloud IDs. on-premise are Microsoft ADFS etc...
- A service provider (SP)/ resource provider (RP) is an app that trusts the IDP. 

Identity Federation flow:
- user accesses app
- App redirects user to identity provider for authentication
- User authenticated successfully, server digitally signs token containing claims. Claims are considered to be a form of attribute based access control(ABAC)
- user is authorized to use app

AWS Identity solutions:
- Single sign on (SSO)
- AWS Security token service (STS): it is solution to allow temporary credentials to be used for IAM users

